<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Delmenhorst</h1><p class="st-meta">1281 CE – 1647 CE (approx.) · capital Delmenhorst</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North German county of a branch of the Oldenburg counts, seized by the Bishop of Münster for 65 years and reunited with Oldenburg in 1647.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> divided their lands in the 13th century, and around 1281 a separate line took Delmenhorst, a castle and town south-west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a>, as its seat. The county lay on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-bremen">Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen</a> and the Oldenburg lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was rural, with marsh and moorland farming. The first Delmenhorst line died out in 1436, and the county returned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a>. In 1482 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-munster">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</a> seized it and held it until Count Anton I of Oldenburg recovered it in 1547. The counts introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>. From 1577 a younger Oldenburg branch under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ii-count-of-delmenhorst">Anton II</a> ruled Delmenhorst again.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the Delmenhorst line died out in 1647, the county was reunited with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">County of Oldenburg</a>. Both passed to the Danish crown in 1667 and later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-oldenburg">Duchy of Oldenburg</a>. Delmenhorst is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-delmenhorst.jpg" alt="County of Delmenhorst" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Charlie1965nrw at German Wikipedia (Original text: Charlie1965nrw (Karl-Heinz Meurer)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delmenhorst_Rathaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1281 CE – 1647 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Delmenhorst</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anton II of Delmenhorst</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Delmenhorst</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1464&amp;focus=county-of-delmenhorst" aria-label="County of Delmenhorst on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4873 351 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Delmenhorst lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5193 607L5183 604"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5183 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5193 607h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5193 607h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1464&amp;focus=county-of-delmenhorst">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
